  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian Humanitarian Partnership (AHP) implements a Disaster READY program in Fiji, Solomon Islands, Papua New Guinea (PNG), Vanuatu and Timor-Leste. In PNG, Plan International established plant nurseries in six communities, involving 47 Disaster Risk Management Committee (DRMC) members, comprised of 35 men and 12 women. The nurseries provide a space in which farmers can learn best practice related to the propagation of hybrid and drought-resistant crops. A focus group discussion held with 3 Ward Councillors from the district expressed their satisfaction with the physical structures of the nurseries and the subsequent motivation of DRMC members to explore adoption of climate-smart crop varieties. The PNG Women in Agriculture Development Foundation are now looking to replicate these nurseries in their own work across the country, supporting communities to be more resilient to the effects of climate change.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian Humanitarian Partnership (AHP) implements the Disaster READY program in Fiji, Solomon Islands, Papua New Guinea, Vanuatu and Timor-Leste. In Timor-Leste, Oxfam provided rainwater tanks to 11 communities, benefitting 166 households. These communities established 11 Water Management Committees, who will work together to oversee the use of community water sources and identify ways to prevent water wastage, both for these new tanks as well as springs and other water sources. The committees also conduct maintenance on pipes and canals to reduce water loss and deliver awareness training to other community members on how to reduce their personal water waste. This is supporting communities to access reliable, clean and sustainable water year- round, making them more resilient to the effects of climate change.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australian Humanitarian Partnership (AHP) implements the Disaster READY program in Fiji, Solomon Islands, Papua New Guinea, Vanuatu and Timor-Leste. In Vanuatu, AHP partner Save the Children piloted a cash transfer initiative via Digicel Mobile Money, supporting the families of 215 children with disabilities or developmental delays from Shefa and Sanma Provinces. In this pilot, cash was disbursed in four monthly transfers and achieved significant measurable impacts across key areas: the percentage of caregivers able to meet their children&#x2019;s essential needs increased substantially from 38% at baseline to 91% at the end of the pilot, while household stress levels among beneficiary families decreased markedly from 52% to 12%. Reported savings capacity also improved from 68% to 93%. The pilot evaluation found the cash transfer intervention had been effective and efficient, supporting households to be more resilient to the effect of disasters.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
